  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/1407fa82fd00d07311cb34c6a3d5f0d0c79808d321d049d74114a6f4f2e5f6a6.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(3,2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,2)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$",
